WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:02.463
There are over 2,000

00:00:02.463 --> 00:00:04.413
individual firefly species,

00:00:04.413 --> 00:00:07.190
all within the taxonomic
family of Lampyridae,

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.450
which is pretty easy to remember.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.770
And these lightning bugs with
their flickering light shows

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:15.270
make summer nights feel all
the more magical and romantic.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.750
But how did fireflies manage

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.600
to catch lightning in a bottle?

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.980
The answer is found in the bug's butt,

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.060
or more specifically in its abdomen,

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.090
in an organ called the lantern.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.750
This organ is a set of
specialized light cells,

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.490
all encased in a translucent exoskeleton.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:33.350
And those light cells are
where the magic happens:

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.600
the phenomenon of bioluminescence,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.810
when a chemical reaction in
a living thing emits light.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.930
Fireflies aren't the only
creatures that have this power.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.480
Glowworms and certain
deep-sea fish species

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.832
are some of the creatures capable

00:00:45.832 --> 00:00:47.790
of producing and emitting light.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.230
But the firefly is probably

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.290
the Earth's most famous
bioluminescent species.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.530
So what's happening inside
the firefly's light cells?

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.491
What's the secret to its glow?

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:02.000
In the 19th century, French
pharmacologist Raphaël Dubois,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.020
working with bioluminescent clams,

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.160
discovered that there are
two essential components

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.960
to these creatures' light show.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.460
He named them luciferin and luciferase,

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:13.490
based on the Latin term
lucifer, for "light-bringer."

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.010
Luciferin is the compound
that generates light,

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.840
and luciferase is the
enzyme that acts on it.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.750
Today, we know that the
firefly's bioluminescent reaction

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.530
plays out like this.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:25.880
A firefly diverts oxygen
to its light cells

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.230
through its tracheoles.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.960
And those oxygen molecules
react to luciferin,

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.060
catalyzed with the help of luciferase

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.390
and energy in the form of ATP.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.170
The luciferin then becomes
agitated and excited,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.970
elevating its energy level.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.420
And when the excited luciferin

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.210
drops back to its normal state,

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.700
it releases that energy
in the form of light,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.120
creating the "fire" in fireflies.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.490
It's a remarkable phenomenon

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.730
that's also remarkably efficient.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.230
In a light bulb, 90%
of the energy consumed

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.713
is given off as heat,

00:01:54.713 --> 00:01:57.405
with only the remaining
energy, a mere 10%,

00:01:57.405 --> 00:01:59.100
given off as visible light.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.930
In a firefly, on the other hand,

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.360
nearly 100% of the energy
is given off as light.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.500
That luminescence, or "cold
light," as it's also called,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.080
is produced in the light cells

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.780
and then focused by a
layer of reflector cells,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.630
which direct that beam outward

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.960
through that translucent exoskeleton.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.396
But why do fireflies do what they do?

00:02:18.396 --> 00:02:19.615
As it turns out,

00:02:19.615 --> 00:02:22.620
bioluminescence has a number
of evolutionary benefits,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.627
helping certain marine species
lure prey to their mouths

00:02:25.627 --> 00:02:28.020
or serving as a defense against predators.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:29.300
Sara Lewis: Fireflies are beetles,

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.410
and so the juvenile
fireflies live underground.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:37.140
So, we think that firefly light
first evolved as a warning.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.607
It's like a neon sign that shouts out,

00:02:39.607 --> 00:02:42.030
"Don't eat me, I'm toxic."

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.710
Narrator: But in adult fireflies,

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.200
the purpose is a bit more romantic.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.120
Those yellow flashes lighting
up our warm summer nights

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.610
are actually part of the
fireflies' complex mating rituals,

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:54.377
with male fireflies attracting

00:02:54.377 --> 00:02:56.575
female fireflies of the same species

00:02:56.575 --> 00:02:59.840
by flashing a distinctive,
recognizable pattern.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.800
So those lights twinkling around you,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.541
switching on and off seemingly at random -

00:03:04.541 --> 00:03:05.941
they're just the opposite:

00:03:05.941 --> 00:03:08.010
a highly intricate, specialized form

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.350
of species-specific seduction.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.445
Lewis: In North America,
males might flash,

00:03:12.445 --> 00:03:14.170
like, just one flash.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.430
Wait, wait, wait, wait,
wait, wait, wait, bleep,

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.730
another flash, wait, wait,
wait, wait, wait, bleep,

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:19.563
another flash.

00:03:19.563 --> 00:03:22.160
Some species, the males
actually give paired flashes,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.973
so they'll fly along and
then go bleep, bleep,

00:03:25.973 --> 00:03:27.708
wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:31.100
Bleep, bleep, wait,
wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:31.933
And so on.

00:03:31.933 --> 00:03:34.990
And so females who are
kind of hanging around

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:38.080
on grass down below can see these flashes,

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.670
and they can recognize a
male of their own species.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.150
Narrator: But for all
the romance and magic

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.850
they add to our summer evenings,

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:47.720
firefly populations around
the globe are at serious risk.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.950
Those finely tuned mating rituals?

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.280
Thanks to light pollution,

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.500
those love letters get a
little lost in translation.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.200
Lewis: In areas where there's
a lot of bright lights,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.398
it's been shown that it's
much, much more difficult

00:04:00.398 --> 00:04:02.720
for the male fireflies to find the females

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.810
and for the females to see the flashes,

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:09.710
the advertisement flashes
of the male fireflies.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.360
Narrator: And other
threats like habitat loss

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.660
and pesticide use have also
put the population at risk.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.020
Lewis: Sadly, in many parts of the world,

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:20.530
there are other firefly species
that aren't doing so well.

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:23.090
In fact, they are flickering out.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:26.140
And some of these fireflies are restricted

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.860
to a very specific habitat.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.040
If that habitat goes away,
the fireflies disappear.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.000
They can't live anywhere else.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.200
Narrator: It's a story playing out

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.330
all over the planet and
across the animal kingdom.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.640
But as Lewis explains,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.940
education is absolutely
key to conservation

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.890
of fireflies and of all at-risk species.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:46.973
Lewis: If fireflies disappeared,

00:04:46.973 --> 00:04:50.381
a lot of the world's wonder

00:04:50.381 --> 00:04:51.930
would disappear with them.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:55.500
Would you wanna live in a
world without fireflies?

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:56.980
I would not.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.476
Narrator: By increasing
awareness of these risk factors,

00:04:59.476 --> 00:05:03.032
Lewis hopes to shine a little
light on firefly conservation,

00:05:03.032 --> 00:05:04.740
ensuring that these little bugs

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.215
will be able to dazzle
us for years to come,

00:05:07.215 --> 00:05:08.895
giving future generations the chance

00:05:08.895 --> 00:05:10.050
to spend their summer nights

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.267
trying to catch lightning in a bottle.

